/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Cut a small element dx × dy from a plate loaded by a pressure q (N/m²) on its face.
   - Now let in-plane forces act as well.
   - w is the deflection (m); D the plate stiffness (N·m); q the lateral pressure (N/m²); Nx, Ny the in-plane direct forces per unit length (N/m), tension positive; Nxy the in-plane shear force per unit length (N/m); ∇⁴w = ∂⁴w/∂x⁴ + 2∂⁴w/∂x²∂y² 
   - With no in-plane force the right side is just q.
   - Navier's method solves a rectangular plate a × b simply supported on all four edges.
   - Amn is the amplitude of the (m, n) term (m); a and b are the plate sides (m); m and n count half-waves along x and y.
   - The first term alone gives 4.15 mm for the plate of the worked example, 2.4% above the full sum: the series converges fast for deflection, more slowly for moments.
   - Levy's method needs only two opposite edges simply supported.
   - For a simply supported plate under a uniform compression Nx = −N, substituting the sine series into the full equation gives each term exactly:
   - Amn(0) is the amplitude without compression; N is the compressive force per unit length (N/m); Nmn is the compression at which the (m, n) shape would stand without any pressure: its buckling load.
*/
