/* book exam traps: Entering a compressive Nx as a positive number in D∇⁴w = q + Nx ∂²w/∂x² + …; tension is positive there, and a wrong sign makes compression appear to stiffen the plate. | Treating Nx as a stress. Nx is a force per unit length (N/m); σx = Nx/t. | Using the coefficient 0.00406 for clamped edges or for a non-square plate. It is for a square plate simply supported on all edges under uniform pressure. | Setting the twisting moment to zero on a free edge. The two free-edge conditions are zero bending moment and zero effective shear Qx + ∂Mxy/∂y. | Checking only stress and ignoring Nx/Ncr. A compression well below yield can still magnify deflection and bending stress, and buckles the skin at Ncr. */
